From 7910a3442e6fce14787e72f3f3ea15714569e997 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 03:36:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(interaction): preserve native held item use

Native Bedrock 1.26.60.5 keeps the first predicted trapdoor facing, half
and world hit during held placement. Preserve that state across trapdoor
materials, temporary item-family changes and server correction. Apply the
snap only while holding a trapdoor. Other block items use fresh hits while
suppressing repeated activation; an empty hand restores native interaction.
Returning to a trapdoor reuses the original snap until physical release.

Carry the use lifetime and sequence-owned prediction format 2 through
direct and ViaProxy sessions. Keep the native cursor separate from the
ordinary Java raycast packet, including coordinates outside later target
cubes. Preserve independent water, power and material properties. Separate
trapdoors and the original support remain eligible while snapped.

Match the 300 ms delay after the first attempt or a successful use. Failed
repeats do not restart it. Poll subsequent attempts from the input frame
after camera movement, before the next game tick publishes rotation.
Suppress Java's parallel four-tick repeat path for that active hold.
This preserves native placement/camera ordering without forcing server
orientation. Release, screens and connection replacement retire the
placement input timer and placement state.

Keep the existing native outline. Fresh native bottom-face hits use
0.8175; the earlier 0.8125 claim came from the Java fixture. Clock-boundary,
item-state and block-state tests cover the retained input state. Private
native, direct and proxy captures establish gameplay behavior separately
from these model tests.

Select the existing native outline and occlusion behavior from the active
Bedrock connection, including remote ViaProxy sessions. Their local Java
target version does not identify the backend. Without this check fresh
proxy hits retained Java's 0.8125 bottom instead of native 0.8175.

Coordinate native food use with the existing placement input owner. Retain
the connection's 200 ms monotonic food deadline separately from placement's
300 ms first-attempt/success delay. Gate fresh calls before packet/local
use, and retry held foods from input frames after the strict deadline.
Keep the food deadline across physical release and item changes; clear it
on connection replacement. Release still clears the held placement timer
and retained trapdoor state. Missing optional block-use channels do not
retire the independent food deadline.

Select food from the transported FOOD component, including custom paper
carriers. Set the deadline before main-hand air use even if hunger later
refuses the request. Canonical consumption and completion stay server-owned.
Other air-use families retain their existing route; a pending food deadline
still gates a subsequent attempt, including a change to a block item.

Native 1.26.60.5/BDS 1.26.60.6 protocol-2225 runtime tracing establishes
the monotonic clock and strict deadline check before main-hand air use. Both
fresh-press and held-frame callers reach that boundary. A 2.1-second
instant-food hold consumes nine items with or without the debugger; the
old Java four-tick path consumes eleven. Three 80 ms clicks consume two
when their starts are about 0,131,616 ms. Input-frame cadence determines
quantization, so do not replace this deadline with a fixed five-tick delay.

Behavioral timing fixtures cover variable input frames, released food,
connection reset, both clock boundaries, rollover and separate food/block
lifetimes during held item changes. Complete component dispatch, other
use families, exact phase counts and the original intermittent food report
remain open; these controls do not establish full native use parity.
